 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; the quiz in <a href="#firing">Hovering, running and diving fire</a> and Figures 1 to 3 cover more.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Is an Army close combat attack the same as close air support?</summary><p>No. CCA is the Army's attack for friendly units in close combat; it needs detailed integration but no terminal control from the ground, and the Army does not consider its attack helicopters a CAS system.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> A Marine section checks in as an AH-1 and a UH-1. Why the mix?</summary><p>Mixed sections give the most flexible mix of sensors, communications, maneuverability, firepower and mutual support.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Contact is possible but not imminent, and speed matters. How do you move?</summary><p>Traveling overwatch, by contour or NOE flight: the main element keeps moving while the overwatch element covers it.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> At 400 ft above the ground, which altitude band are you in, and is it a terrain flight profile?</summary><p>Low, below 500 ft. It is not one of the terrain flight profiles' normal heights: low level is normally 100 to 200 ft, contour 50 to 100 ft.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> The brief says "Oak, 060, 3400". What do the three items mean?</summary><p>Line 1, the battle position Oak; line 2, the heading in degrees magnetic from its center to the target, 060; line 3, the distance from its center, 3,400 meters, given to the nearest 100.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Who picks your firing point, and what do you do there before the attack?</summary><p>You do, once in the battle position, after the controller's or mission commander's final instructions; you stay masked until the time on target, time to target or the order to attack.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Army. The commander calls "SET HOT". What does that tell you, and how long should the company stay in one battle position?</summary><p>Every team is in position with targets in sight ("SET COLD" means in position without them). As a rule, no more than 10 minutes in any one battle position.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Why might a hover make your rockets less accurate?</summary><p>A hovering aircraft can be less stable; forward flight may add stability and improve the accuracy of unguided ordnance.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> What does the overhead give you over a city, and what does it cost?</summary><p>High situational awareness, a view down into the streets, a constant weapons solution and more accurate fire; but continuous exposure, and an easier shot for an infrared SAM.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> Can you fire Hellfire without seeing the target?</summary><p>Yes: lock-on after launch, with a remote designator lasing the target, lets you fire from a masked position with longer standoff.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> After the attack, how do you choose the egress altitude and airspeed?</summary><p>By the same considerations as the inbound en route phase: the threat, terrain and formation; then rearm and refuel at a FARP if you can, and pass BDA and a mission report.</p></details>

