 <p id="source-jp"><strong>[1] US Joint Chiefs of Staff, JP 3-09.3, Close Air Support, 25 November 2014.</strong> <a href="https://www.bits.de/NRANEU/others/jp-doctrine/jp3_09_3(14).pdf">Read on the Berlin Information-center for Transatlantic Security's copy</a>, 10 November 2026, by printed page: rotary-wing considerations pp. I-5 to I-6 and I-8; Hellfire pp. III-59 to III-61; low-altitude fixed-wing coordination p. III-87; rotary-wing CAS tactics pp. III-89 to III-94; the 5-line pp. III-94 to III-95; off-axis weapons p. V-11; the 9-line for helicopters pp. V-26 and V-32; Example 7 pp. E-17 to E-19. A later edition may differ.</p>
 <p id="source-jp102"><strong>[2] US Department of Defense, JP 1-02, Dictionary of Military and Associated Terms, 8 November 2010, as amended through 15 January 2016.</strong> <a href="https://schoolresources.militaryfamilies.psu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/department_of_defense_dictionary_of_military_and_associated_terms.pdf">PDF</a>, read 10 November 2026: defilade p. 63; terrain flight p. 240.</p>
 <p id="source-fm304"><strong>[3] Headquarters, Department of the Army, FM 3-04, Army Aviation, 6 April 2020.</strong> Approved for public release. <a href="https://aviation-assets.info/wp-content/uploads/ARN21797_FM_3-04_FINAL_WEB_wfix.pdf">PDF</a>, read 10 November 2026, by printed page: attacks pp. 3-3 to 3-8; planning and methods of employment pp. 3-11 to 3-14. Army-specific.</p>
 <p id="source-fm126"><strong>[4] Headquarters, Department of the Army, FM 3-04.126, Attack Reconnaissance Helicopter Operations, 16 February 2007.</strong> Approved for public release. <a href="https://aviation-assets.info/wp-content/uploads/fm-3-04-126-attack-reconnaissance-helicopter-operations.pdf">PDF</a>, read 10 November 2026, by printed page: hasty attack planning p. 3-19; static attack, firing techniques and attack methods pp. 3-68 to 3-70; engagements, holding areas and attack by fire and battle position operations pp. 3-83 to 3-86; offset positions p. B-3. Army-specific and superseded; later Army publications may differ.</p>
 <h3>Sim sources</h3>
 <p id="source-ah64"><strong>[5] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: AH-64D Flight Manual (English).</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/upload/iblock/30e/j1dh7g3egpennjdngtub7uubwcqfgrrh/DCS%20AH-64D%20Flight%20Manual%20EN.pdf">PDF</a>, read 10 November 2026, pp. 350–354, 559 and 578. A sim manual, cited for the In the sim panel and Figure 2's NOE height only.</p>
 <p id="source-ka50"><strong>[6] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: Black Shark 3 Flight Manual (English).</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/upload/iblock/ece/ak5ecs04jakt38ubluxzfjqpv5k3zp5p/DCS_BS3_Flight_Manual_EN.pdf">PDF</a>, read 10 November 2026, pp. 11–1 to 11–2. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/rotary-wing-attack-evidence.md">Rotary-Wing Attack evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1 to 3 and the worked attack are original, invented examples. Figure 1 is laid out like the joint publication's Figure III-26; Figure 2's ground and line-of-sight are our geometry.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for helicopter aircrew of every service who attack ground targets, and the controllers and ground units they support. The procedures are from the joint close air support publication, which all four services follow; Army and Marine Corps differences are labelled. The Army section uses the capstone FM 3-04 and the superseded FM 3-04.126, the last attack reconnaissance manual in a public copy; its current tactics publications (ATP 3-04.1, TC 3-04.12) could not be read, and restricted training circulars are not used. Theater SPINS, your unit's tactics and your aircraft's manual govern.</p>
